6136 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1045 "Speed as a Unit Rate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4cf8b9a9-4050-5d59-b451-2f66b157c8dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176332 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176332 #edb/ref 1071 "It takes $20$ minutes for a slug to crawl $90$ centimeters at a constant pace. How much time does it take for the slug to crawl $450$ centimeters?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "259c2553-9228-546d-90f1-165de32084d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176820 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176820 #edb/ref 1061 36386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176820 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "136b294f-0279-574d-9755-cfc8cb54f15d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176820 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176821 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176821 #edb/ref 1061 36387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176821 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aed82a50-e10d-539e-895f-797eb5220670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176821 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176822 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176822 #edb/ref 1061 36388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176822 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e52190a-967b-5eee-8781-4b1c6cdc1bf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176822 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176823 #edb/ref 1061 36389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176823 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb434a73-533d-5a74-927b-b8b1bf69153e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176823 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176824 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176824 #edb/ref 1061 36390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176824 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02f9ad99-d474-59a2-b870-d4cc8af16598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176824 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176825 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176825 #edb/ref 1061 36391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176825 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1aec8595-c434-5eab-92bb-a1d9a3d99a15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176825 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177044 #edb/ref 1064 "**Speed** is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*.\n\nWe can calculate the speed of a moving object using the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a car travels $80$ miles in $2$ hours at a constant rate. What is the speed of the car? \n\nTo find the speed (in miles per hour), we divide the number of miles, $80,$ by the number of hours, $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{80}{2} = 40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $40$ miles per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177044 #edb/ref 1065 5959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e151f782-1f59-5438-92a6-e678c9041d99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177044 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177045 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's return to the example of a car traveling at ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles per hour. \n\nIf the car travels at this speed, it covers  a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles *every hour*. Therefore, we can deduce the following:\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ hour, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{1}} = 40$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{2}} = 80$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{3}} = 120$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{4}} = 160$ miles\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nThis suggests the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the distance traveled by an object moving at a constant speed.\n\nFor instance, suppose an elevator traveled for $1.5$ minutes at a constant speed of $120$ feet per minute. What distance is covered by the elevator?\n\nThe speed is $120$ feet per minute. To find the distance covered in $1.5$ minutes, we multiply the speed by $1.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =120\\times 1.5 \\\\  & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elevator covered a distance of $180$ feet." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177045 #edb/ref 1065 5960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177045 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "95f37ad7-7647-57e9-ae85-7a3dcab40f25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177045 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Distance Traveled by an Object" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177046 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recall the formula relating distance to speed and time:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} \\times \\text{time} = \\text{distance}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write this equation as\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\text{distance} \\div \\text{speed}\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the time taken for an object moving at a particular speed to cover a certain distance. \n\nFor example, suppose a plane flew for $3\\,120$ kilometers at a constant speed of $520$ kilometers per hour. For how long was the plane in motion?\n\nTo determine the time the plane was in motion, we divide the distance, $3\\,120$ kilometers, by the speed, $520$ kilometers per hour. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,120}{520} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,120÷10}{520÷10} \\\\  & =\\frac{312}{52} \\\\  & =312÷52 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane was in motion for $6$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177046 #edb/ref 1065 5961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177046 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "76064dfb-b2e4-56a5-95f7-2a97b1fb392b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177046 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Total Time an Object is in Motion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266366 #edb/ref 1080 "$30$ miles per hour" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266366 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a220e8a-774f-53a7-b89b-3acdf479891e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266412 #edb/ref 1080 "$234$ kilometers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266412 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266412 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ef48712-83ca-543d-a0c9-9799b7aeb83e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266675 #edb/ref 1080 "$14$ feet per month" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266675 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266675 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3c860c0c-180d-5348-9a89-50590ef2af0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266680 #edb/ref 1080 "$6.5$ minutes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266680 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266680 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3cab5046-233a-5880-a03b